# Inter-VLAN Routing


## Objective
Enable communication between different VLANs using a router (Router-on-a-Stick method).

## Tools Used
- Cisco Packet Tracer
- Cisco Router
- Cisco Switch
- PCs
- Ethernet Cables

## Theory
Inter-VLAN Routing allows devices in different VLANs to communicate with each other. 
This is commonly implemented using a router with subinterfaces connected to a switch trunk port.

## Network Example
VLAN 10 → 192.168.10.0/24  
VLAN 20 → 192.168.20.0/24  

## Procedure

### Step 1: Create VLANs on Switch

Switch> enable
Switch# configure terminal

vlan 10
name SALES
exit

vlan 20
name HR
exit


### Step 2: Assign Ports to VLANs

interface fastEthernet 0/1
switchport mode access
switchport access vlan 10
exit

interface fastEthernet 0/2
switchport mode access
switchport access vlan 20
exit


### Step 3: Configure Trunk Port

interface fastEthernet 0/24
switchport mode trunk
exit


### Step 4: Configure Router Subinterfaces

Router> enable
Router# configure terminal

interface fastEthernet0/0.10
encapsulation dot1Q 10
ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.0
exit

interface fastEthernet0/0.20
encapsulation dot1Q 20
ip address 192.168.20.1 255.255.255.0
exit

interface fastEthernet0/0
no shutdown


### Step 5: Verify Configuration

show ip interface brief
show vlan brief


